<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sami al-Hinnawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1898–1950</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a> al-Hinnawi was a Syrian army officer who led the second military coup of 1949, overthrowing and executing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husni-al-zaim">Husni al-Za'im</a> in August. He restored civilian government but favoured union with Hashemite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, which alarmed other officers, and in December 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adib-shishakli">Adib Shishakli</a> removed him. He went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, where he was assassinated in 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sami-al-hinnawi.jpg" alt="Sami al-Hinnawi" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sami_al-Hinnawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
